extends /templates/code-article.pg

append head
  title Code reviewing lone lisp with Sol and Fable
  meta(name=description content='Comparative code review of the lone lisp interpreter source code by Sol and Fable.')
  meta(name=keywords content='ai, review, code review, benchmark, claude, fable, gpt-5.6, sol, lone lisp')

block article

  h1 Code reviewing lone lisp with Sol and Fable

  p.
    I've been a solo programmer for over ten years.
    It is so wonderful to be able to work on my own
    projects with no pressure of any kind, and just
    make real the things I envision with my mind.
    It's always been a recreational, even spiritual,
    activity for me.

  p.
    I definitely feel the isolation though.
    Writing code for no one in particular,
    just for myself. Never following anyone
    else's standards. Never knowing what my
    peers thought. Did I even have peers?

  p.
    LLMs changed that.

  p.
    I paid for my Anthropic subscription
    in early 2026. Chose Anthropic just
    because I had vaguely heard it was
    the more ethical of the AI companies.
    The first thing I did was connect my
    GitHub account and synchronize all my
    repositories, then ask Claude Opus 4.6
    for a full code review of @[lone].
    I was immediately impressed, and
    code review became a mainstay of
    my hobby software development
    ever since. I had a coding buddy
    now.

  h2 The ultimate code review

  p.
    Anthropic had already released its newest, most powerful
    model, Claude Mythos Preview, as far back as April this
    year. Only a few "trusted" corporations and institutions
    were given access at first, and peasants such as myself
    needed not apply.

  p.
    A version of Mythos called Fable
    @[fable-launch was finally released]
    to the public on #(time(datetime='2026-06-09') June 9th),
    and I began a complete code review of lone lisp
    on #(time(datetime='2026-06-10') June 10th),
    the very next day. I wanted to see
    what the world's smartest AI model
    had to say about @[lone].

  p.
    That was the beginning of the end
    of my business relationship with
    Anthropic. @[fable] launched with
    incredibly obnoxious safeguards
    that added enormous friction.
    It'd often downgrade to Opus
    while reviewing lone, especially
    the parts that needed it the most!
    In spite of these safeguards, the US
    government @[fable-suspension imposed export controls]
    on #(time(datetime='2026-06-12') June 12th),
    causing Anthropic to suspend access.
    @[fable-redeployment Service was only restored]
    on #(time(datetime='2026-07-01') July 1st).

  p.
    Anthropic is extremely stingy with Fable.
    There is a separate meter for it, and on
    subscriptions it has higher multipliers
    compared to API prices. One 5h code review
    window would take out 10% of my weekly
    quota, and that's the happy path where
    Fable wouldn't downgrade to Opus.

  p.
    I nevertheless slogged through this review
    out of sheer stubbornness. It took _(over
    a month) to complete this code review!
    My subscription ended just in time...

  p.
    Subscribed to OpenAI that same day,
    and the grass turned out to be much
    greener here. @[sol] had just come out,
    and I had started to wonder if it was
    really as good as Fable... Let's see!

  table#code-review-scale
    caption Scale of the Sol and Fable code reviews
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Reviewer
        th.number(scope='col') Agent invocations
        th.number(scope='col') Agent-hours
        th.number(scope='col') Input tokens
        th.number(scope='col') Output tokens
        th.number(scope='col') API-equivalent cost
    tbody
      tr
        td Fable
        td.number 702
        td.number 66.271
        td.number 635,263,296
        td.number 14,216,818
        td.number $1,757.80
      tr
        td Sol
        td.number 257
        td.number 46.526
        td.number 773,216,978
        td.number 5,366,778
        td.number $523.32
      tr
        td Both
        td.number 959
        td.number 112.797
        td.number 1,408,480,274
        td.number 19,583,596
        td.number $2,281.12

  p.
    Fable's invocation numbers are higher due to
    Anthropic's very low usage limits, as well as
    cybersecurity blocks and their associated retries.

  p.
    It costs two thousand two hundred dollars
    to review lone lisp... For twelve weeks.

  p.
    Thank god we have subscriptions.

  h2 The review skill

  p.
    I used my own @[scrutinize `(scrutinize)]
    parallel code review skill which works by
    applying "critics" to "subsystems".

  p.
    The skill currently defines fourteen "critics",
    which are agent personas that review the code
    from various perspectives. Twelve were used
    during this review.

  table#review-lenses
    caption The 12 review lenses
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Critic lens
        th(scope='col') Driving question
    tbody
      tr
        td `(quality)
        td How high quality is this, in general?
      tr
        td `(correctness)
        td Is this implemented correctly?
      tr
        td `(rigor)
        td Is this the real, complete fix? Or is it a hack?
      tr
        td `(integration)
        td Does this work well with everything else?
      tr
        td `(design)
        td Is this the right way to implement it?
      tr
        td `(consistency)
        td Does this follow the codebase's established conventions?
      tr
        td `(maintainability)
        td Will this remain understandable and changeable over time?
      tr
        td `(duplication)
        td Are things repeating themselves needlessly?
      tr
        td `(performance)
        td.
          Is it efficient? Does it use optimal
          algorithms and data structures?
      tr
        td `(tests)
        td Do the tests encode correct behavior and catch real failures?
      tr
        td `(security)
        td Can untrusted input provoke unintended behavior?
      tr
        td `(robustness)
        td.
          Do hostile or extreme inputs terminate the program,
          stay bounded or fail early?

  p.
    The review coordinator explores the repository
    and subdivides it into subsystems suitable for
    review. This limits the scope and keeps context
    manageable.

  table#lone-lisp-subdivision
    caption Lone lisp subdivided into 21 subsystems
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Subsystem
        th(scope='col') Responsibility
    tbody
      tr
        td `(architecture)
        td Entry points and raw system call ABI functions
      tr
        td `(c-tests-lisp)
        td C tests of tagged Lisp values and constants
      tr
        td `(c-tests-reader-primitives)
        td C tests of the reader and low level primitives
      tr
        td `(module-text)
        td Intrinsic text operations
      tr
        td `(modules-lone)
        td Core-language intrinsic primitives
      tr
        td `(modules-math-list)
        td Arithmetic and list intrinsics
      tr
        td `(stack-bits-types)
        td Generic stack, bitmaps, endian types and Unicode
      tr
        td `(system-foundation)
        td Process startup, Linux, memory allocator and randomness
      tr
        td `(test-framework)
        td C test results and assertion framework
      tr
        td `(module-bytes)
        td Mutable and frozen byte buffer intrinsics
      tr
        td `(build-scripts)
        td Makefile, system call generation and test harness
      tr
        td `(modules-vector-table)
        td Vector and table intrinsics
      tr
        td `(hash-elf)
        td Hashing and ELF parsing
      tr
        td `(gc)
        td.
          Garbage collector that marks, sweeps, compacts
          and spills registers
      tr
        td `(lisp-types)
        td Tagged types, the heap, value hashing and utilities
      tr
        td `(lisp-values)
        td Concrete heap value implementations of each lisp type
      tr
        td `(module-linux)
        td Linux `(system-call) intrinsics and process data
      tr
        td `(reader-printer)
        td The lisp parser and printer
      tr
        td `(machine)
        td The virtual lisp machine
      tr
        td `(module-system)
        td.
          Module system, module loading, `(import),
          embedded modules, intrinsic module initialization
      tr
        td `(lone-embed)
        td Custom made ELF patcher tool

  p.
    12 critics multiplied by 21 subsystems
    results in a 252 cell code review matrix.
    Multiplied by the two frontier models,
    the result is 504 individual review cells.
    It's not exactly a controlled experiment,
    but I think it still turned out pretty good.

  p.
    All reviewers go in blind with no prior
    context outside of their prompts.
    No list of known issues is provided.

  p.
    Agents run in parallel and commit their findings
    into an orphan `(ai) branch in the git repository.
    This _(easily) burns even the most generous token
    allowances!

  p.
    A filesystem journal style algorithm
    was used to deal with the interruptions
    caused by the five hour limits: agents
    would keep a journal and redo operations
    that got interrupted and didn't complete.
    To deal with safety lockouts, the skill
    also encoded a GNU ddrescue style algorithm:
    complete all easy cells first, then scrape
    any blocked cells by retrying with modified
    prompts until they succeed. This squeezed
    every last drop of juice out of Fable even
    in spite of Anthropic's safety classifiers.

  p.
    These agents produced _(a lot) of output. One finding
    can contain several problems, and multiple agents can
    find the same issues independently. When done, the review
    coordinator goes through the findings and semantically
    splits and deduplicates them all. Multiple independent
    findings increase confidence. Agents classify the severity
    of findings and then results converge into what's essentially
    a titanic TODO list of issues.

  p.
    It's these results that I'm going to use as a measure
    of the performance of Sol and Fable.

  h2 The results

  p.
    The agents produced 538 `(HIGH)/`(CRITICAL) findings
    which were split into 637 atomic claims and further
    deduplicated into 232 unique semantic items, one of
    which was later rejected. I excluded 63 missing
    test findings, 10 maintainability or duplication
    recommendations and 5 documentation findings from
    the tables. This left me with 153 unique defects.

  table#findings-by-severity
    caption.
      Maximum code review severity among 153 unique defects
      derived from `(HIGH)/`(CRITICAL) findings
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Severity
        th.number(scope='col') Fable
        th.number(scope='col') Sol
    tbody
      tr
        td `(CRITICAL)
        td.number 4
        td.number 3
      tr
        td `(HIGH)
        td.number 66
        td.number 126
      tr
        td `(MEDIUM)
        td.number 25
        td.number 12
      tr
        td `(LOW)
        td.number 18
        td.number 1
      tr
        td `(WARNING)
        td.number 1
        td.number 1
      tr
        td `(INFO)
        td.number 4
        td.number 0
      tr
        td Unspecified
        td.number 1
        td.number 0
      tr
        td Total
        td.number 119
        td.number 143

  p.
    Sol found all four unique defects that Fable classified
    as `(CRITICAL). It just happened to classify two of them
    as `(HIGH) instead. Conversely, Fable also found defects
    that Sol classified as `(CRITICAL), but classified one of
    them as `(LOW). Their union contains five unique defects,
    all shared between both code reviews.

  h3 Semantic overlap

  p.
    How many defects were found by both models?
    How many were unique to each model?

  table#finding-overlap
    caption.
      Overlap among 153 unique defects
      derived from `(HIGH)/`(CRITICAL)
      findings
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Found by
        th.number(scope='col') Defects
    tbody
      tr
        td Fable only
        td.number 10
      tr
        td Sol only
        td.number 34
      tr
        td Fable ∩ Sol
        td.number 109
      tr
        td Fable ∪ Sol
        td.number 153

  p.
    Sol corroborated 109 of Fable's 119 defects, or 91.60%,
    but missed 10. Fable corroborated 109 of Sol's 143 defects,
    or 76.22%, but missed 34.

  h3 Known issues rediscovered

  p.
    The project already had an independent list of known
    issues before either code review. Neither model saw it.
    After the review was completed, I compared the findings
    against the 106 pre-existing issues on the list, all of
    which were still present in the source code.

  table#known-issue-rediscovery
    caption Rediscovery of 106 pre-existing and live issues
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Rediscovered by
        th.number(scope='col') Known issues
    tbody
      tr
        td Fable ∩ Sol
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 72.64%') 77
      tr
        td Fable ∪ Sol
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 91.51%') 97
      tr
        td Fable
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 83.96%') 89
      tr
        td Sol
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 80.19%') 85
      tr
        td Fable only
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 11.32%') 12
      tr
        td Sol only
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 7.55%') 8
      tr
        td Neither model
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 8.49%') 9

  p.
    The observed differences were not statistically significant.
    Paired McNemar test `(p = 0.503).

  h3 The grass wasn't always greener

  p.
    Anthropic became famous for its incredibly
    sensitive Fable safety classifier, but OpenAI
    turned out to not be much better. Security
    reviews mostly worked, but robustness reviews
    hit walls _(immediately).

  table#defensive-review-outcomes
    caption First-attempt outcomes for defensive reviews
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Model
        th(scope='col') Lens
        th.number(scope='col') Complete
        th.number(scope='col') Cybersecurity block
        th.number(scope='col') General unavailability
    tbody
      tr
        td Fable
        td Security
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 52.38%') 11/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 14.29%') 3/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 33.33%') 7/21
      tr
        td Fable
        td Robustness
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 66.67%') 14/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 23.81%') 5/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 9.52%') 2/21
      tr
        td Sol
        td Security
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 80.95%') 17/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 19.05%') 4/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0/21
      tr
        td Sol
        td Robustness
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 4.76%') 1/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 95.24%') 20/21
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0/21

  p.
    For Fable, a cybersecurity failure is inferred
    from downgrades to Opus. The skill directs the
    model to detect this and abort rather than
    continue reviewing with a weaker model.
    For Sol, it is a `(cyber_policy) termination.
    General unavailability comprises usage exhaustion,
    timeouts and overloaded compute capacity.

  p.
    The security/robustness prompts already differed initially,
    and diverged further during the code review due to retries.
    Both models eventually produced finished review output for
    all 42 defensive code review cells after enough retries.
    Exploit vocabulary had to be beaten out of the models as
    though the words were unforgivable wrongthink. This means
    the reports were less directly comparable, but the first
    attempt table is still a good measure of how much friction
    these safeguards add.

  p.
    These 42 defensive assignments were only one sixth
    of the review matrix, but produced 24 of OpenAI's
    36 `(cyber_policy) blocks: exactly two thirds.

  p.
    They weren't the only ones though. There were,
    in total, 50 downgrade events across Fable's
    702 attempts and 36 cybersecurity blocks across
    Sol's 257 attempts, including their retries.

  table#blocks-by-lens
    caption Cybersecurity events in attempts by review lens
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Lens
        th.number(scope='col') Fable downgraded
        th.number(scope='col') Sol blocked
    tbody
      tr
        td `(quality)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 25%') 5
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 5%') 1
      tr
        td `(correctness)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 65%') 13
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 25%') 5
      tr
        td `(rigor)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 30%') 6
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 10%') 2
      tr
        td `(integration)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 25%') 5
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 20%') 4
      tr
        td `(design)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 5%') 1
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(consistency)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 5%') 1
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(maintainability)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(duplication)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(performance)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(tests)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(security)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 45%') 9
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 20%') 4
      tr
        td `(robustness)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 50%') 10
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 100%') 20

  p.
    The high number of events in `(correctness)
    and `(rigor) is highly disappointing, since
    these are the most valuable code reviewers.

  table#blocks-by-subsystem
    caption Cybersecurity events in attempts by subsystem
    thead
      tr
        th(scope='col') Subsystem
        th.number(scope='col') Fable downgraded
        th.number(scope='col') Sol blocked
    tbody
      tr
        td `(architecture)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 8.33%') 1
      tr
        td `(c-tests-lisp)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
      tr
        td `(c-tests-reader-primitives)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 8.33%') 1
      tr
        td `(module-text)
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 0%') 0
        td.number.bar(style='--bar: 8.33%') 1
      tr
